  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 AM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nCurrently, a narrow band of light rain is exiting the sunrise side \nand slowly continuing eastward over Lk Huron. Scattered showers over \ncentral Lk MI and the L.P. are blossoming, but remain very weak. A \nLLJ over IA/WI is pointed towards northern WI this morning, and will \ncontinue to produce scattered showers and a couple storms through \nsunrise with some of these drifting into NW lower. Near the surface, \nobservations show the warm frontal boundary stalled near M-55 as \neast winds over northern lower reinforce 60F temps off of northern \nLk Hurons cooler surface. \n\nZooming out, an upper closed low continues to trek across the \ninternational border by ND. As this whole system continues eastward \nthis morning, it will re-orient the LLJ with the resulting direction \npointed towards northern MI after sunrise. Although the LLJ will be \ntrending weaker as it reaches farther north, it will aid in precip \ngeneration. Very deep moisture will continue to reside over the \nstate today, with PWATs approaching 2 inches. \n\nConfidence is higher for widespread rain/showers today as a better \nsurge of the warm sector, in the right direction, will provide the \nisentropic upglide needed for precip generation. The larger system \ngetting closer helps direct the jet in the right direction, whereas \nyesterday it was more west/east orientated and laying across the \nsouthern portion of the state. Model soundings depict marginal \nelevated instabilty, which would result in embedded showers with \nsome thunder. The environment is just too saturated for severe \nthreats this morning, however heavier rain could be seen under \nthese storms. A deep WCL will be present, yielding the potential\nfor very efficient rain processes. Steering flow continues to \nbe from west to east. Nailing down where the resulting swaths of\ngreater rainfall will be is the challenge. The warm front will \nlift today, reaching at least M-32 by this afternoon. This is \ndriven by the LLJ moving over northern MI later this morning, \nsurging the warm sector northward. Due to the movement of these \nfeatures, rainfall should be spread over much of northern lower \ntoday instead of focused over smaller/narrower areas. An \nadditional quarter to half inch of rain over much of northern \nlower is expected to fall today, with swaths of 0.5 to 1\" under\nthe storms producing heavy rain. \n\nAs the warm front surges northward, temperatures will warm to above \nnormal today under south winds. South winds will become breezy near \nthe Lk MI coast south of Sleeping Bear Dunes late this morning. \n\nLate this evening, the upper closed low will continue east, dragging \na dry slot over northern MN and Lk Superior. Ahead of this, an \nincrease in elevated instability aligns over northern WI and Lk MI. \nIsolated stronger storms will be possible in this environment. \nTonight, this elevated instability will migrate over northern lower. \nMost of the favorable environmental conditions for stronger storms \nwill reside in the mid levels.. shear is marginal with better 0-6km \npresent, and mid level lapse rates are 7 C/km vs 5 C/km in the lower \nlevels. Convection initiated upstream should be able to continue \neast into northern MI as the more favorable environment moves with \nit. Damaging winds, heavy rain, and lightning are the main hazards \nexpected with stronger storms. \n\nTuesday, the dry slot reaches a good portion of northern MI. Shower \nand storm chances continue down state, and could reach up near \nGladwin county. \n\nWednesday, a refocusing of the upper jet over the central rockies \nwill induce another lee cyclone over the plains. The subtropical jet \ncontinues to pump moisture into the desert southwest and over the \ncentral Rockies. More over spreading of moisture is likely mid week \nfor the Great Lakes region, quickly returning clouds and rain \nchances. Temperatures remain warmer than normal.",
